.TH LATER 1 2026-10-30 dotfiles
.ds AD l
.ad l
.ss 12 0
.SH NAME
later \- run a command once at a given time
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B later
.RB [ \-\- ]
.I time command
.RI [ arg ]...
.br
.B later \-l
.br
.BI "later \-c " id
.br
.B later \-h
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B later
runs
.I command
with its
.I arg
operands once at
.IR time ,
as a transient systemd user timer,
and prints the job's
.I id
(six hexadecimal digits)
and the time it will run.
.PP
.I time
is any date string that
.BR date (1)
accepts, given as one argument,
so it must be quoted when it contains spaces.
It must lie in the future.
For example:
.TP 20
.B 09:00
today at 09:00
.TP
.B "\[aq]tomorrow 09:00\[aq]"
tomorrow at 09:00
.TP
.B "\[aq]fri 09:00\[aq]"
the coming Friday, or today on a Friday
.TP
.B "\[aq]next fri 09:00\[aq]"
the same, but a week later on a Friday
.TP
.B "\[aq]2026\-11\-03 14:30\[aq]"
3 November 2026 at 14:30
.TP
.B "\[aq]nov 3 14:30\[aq]"
the same;
.B "\[aq]3 nov 14:30\[aq]"
is not accepted
.TP
.B "\[aq]now + 20 minutes\[aq]"
20 minutes from now
.PP
.I command
runs in the current directory,
with the environment of the systemd user manager
rather than that of the calling shell.
Its standard output and standard error go to the user journal.
.PP
Each job is the systemd unit
.BI later\- id\c
\&.
A job that falls due while the system is suspended
runs when the system resumes.
Queued jobs do not survive a reboot.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-list
List queued jobs, soonest first:
the
.IR id ,
the time each will run, and its command.
The headings are underlined when standard output is a terminal.
Print nothing when no job is queued.
A job whose command is running is no longer queued.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-cancel =\c
.I id
Cancel the queued job
.IR id .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display a summary of usage and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-
End the options;
the next argument is
.I time
even if it begins with a hyphen.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The job was scheduled, listed or cancelled.
.TP
.B 1
.I time
could not be read or has passed,
no job
.I id
is queued,
or systemd failed.
.TP
.B 2
The command line is invalid.
.SH EXAMPLES
Send a reminder at nine tomorrow morning:
.PP
.EX
later \[aq]tomorrow 09:00\[aq] notify\-send standup
.EE
.PP
Run a pipeline in twenty minutes as job 4f2a9c,
then read its output once it has run:
.PP
.EX
later \[aq]now + 20 minutes\[aq] sh \-c \[aq]make 2>&1 | tail \-n 1\[aq]
journalctl \-\-user \-u later\-4f2a9c
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR date (1),
.BR journalctl (1),
.BR systemd\-run (1),
.BR systemd.timer (5)
